### Is there an existing issue for this?

- [X] I have searched the existing open and closed issues

### Current Behavior

Hello! I think I have found a bug in the handling of completed downloads in qBittorrent.

I have set a seed ratio target of 1.1.

[This line](https://github.com/Sonarr/Sonarr/blob/develop/src/NzbDrone.Core/Download/Clients/QBittorrent/QBittorrent.cs#L640) seems to be wrong.

From actual data of one of my torrents, try running this script:
```c#
using System;

public class HelloWorld
{
    private static float torrentRatioLimit = -2f;
    private static float torrentRatio = 1.1006066990976857f;
    
    private static bool configMaxRatioEnabled = true;
    private static float configMaxRatio = 1.1f;
    
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(HasReachedSeedLimit());
    }
    
    public static bool HasReachedSeedLimit()
        {
            if (torrentRatioLimit >= 0)
            {
                if (torrentRatio >= torrentRatioLimit)
                {
                    return true;
                }
            }
            else if (torrentRatioLimit == -2 && configMaxRatioEnabled)
            {
                if (Math.Round(torrentRatio, 2) >= configMaxRatio)
                {
                    return true;
                }
            }

            return false;
        }
}
```

If you run this, you'll see that the output is `false`: https://www.programiz.com/online-compiler/2RZorhtIzCr3v

This is because the [`Math.Round()`](https://learn.microsoft.com/it-it/dotnet/api/system.math.round?view=net-8.0#system-math-round(system-double-system-int32)) function being called is silently converting the value to a `double`.

Given that the value `1.1` is not not representable in floating point, this leads to an unexpected result when comparing a ~1.1 value in float and a ~1.1 in double:

```c#
Console.WriteLine(1.1 >= 1.1f) // this prints false
```
https://www.programiz.com/online-compiler/7gfNQKoM5uzAf

More information on this stack overflow thread: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/6722293/why-comparing-double-and-float-leads-to-unexpected-result


This has lead to all my torrent who have reached the seed ratio and stopped to not being considered by the completed download handler and thus forcing me to manually clean them up.

After discovering this I've manually set the max ratio to `1.1` in one of the stuck torrents, and it got removed almost immediately.

----

Why is that  `Math.Round` call even there? Shouldn't a simple `torrent.Ratio >= config.MaxRatio` suffice? That's also what is done [a few lines above](https://github.com/Sonarr/Sonarr/blob/develop/src/NzbDrone.Core/Download/Clients/QBittorrent/QBittorrent.cs#L633) in the case where the max ratio is defined at the torrent level, and it seems to work fine.

Edit: I see it was added in [this commit](https://github.com/Sonarr/Sonarr/commit/c6bb6ad8788fb1c20ed466a495f2b47034947145), but no explanation on the why.

### Expected Behavior

The torrent is removed.

### Steps To Reproduce

- Set a global share ratio limit of `1.1` in qBittorrent
- Don't set any per-torrent share ratio limit
- Wait for the download to complete, the share ratio to be reached, and qBittorrent to automatically pause the download
- Observe that Sonarr doesn't automatically remove the download, even if the setting is enabled

### Environment

```markdown
- OS: Debain 12
- Sonarr: 4.0.11.2680
- Docker Install: Yes (`lscr.io/linuxserver/sonarr`)
- Using Reverse Proxy: Yes (NGINX Proxy Manager) 
- Browser: Not UI related
- Database: Sqlite 3.47.1
```


### What branch are you running?

Main

### Trace Logs?

[sonarr.trace.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/18269924/sonarr.trace.txt)


### Anything else?

It was quite hard to debug this issue, even with trace log enabled. It would be nice to add more trace logs to understand why a decision is being made. For instance, a log that says `torrent X has seed ratio of X, target of Y not reached`  would have helped immensely in getting to the root cause quicker.
